{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786730569,"nonce":"Hy4ItMcqyMJI6qVKhPf83oYlidE=","title":"**What is wrong.** `QueueReconcile` runs two workers — `internal/platform/jobs/client.go:36`, `QueueReconcile: 2` — and every job the reconcile fan-out enqueues lands on it, at the same kind and priority as the per-source passes that dial a customer's Alertmanager or Prometheus. Those passes are network-bound and may hold a worker for the full sixty seconds `source.reconcile` is registered with (`internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:211`, `Timeout: 60 * time.Second`), against a tick that repeats every thirty (`internal/app/workers.go`, `reconcileFanOutInterval`). Two slow upstreams are therefore enough to stall both remaining slots, and the tenant walk queues behind the same two workers as the work it schedules.","message":"The ceiling this produces is roughly thirty tenants, and it is not a consequence of commit `3124539` — it predates the fan-out conversion and is about seven times tighter than anything the tenant walk itself costs. What `3124539` changed is that the ceiling is now the only thing bounding the work: the old unbounded `Scopes()` loop was killed by its own sixty-second timeout partway down the tenant list, which truncated the walk and, as a side effect, throttled how many per-source jobs were ever enqueued. Removing the truncation — which is the defect that ticket fixed — also removed the accidental brake.\n\n**The demonstration.** Arithmetic from the code, not a load test; it is written into `Reconciler.FanOut`'s doc comment at `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:663-673`. Two workers across a thirty-second tick is sixty worker-seconds of drain per tick. The tenant walk costs almost none of it — 501 rows at roughly three milliseconds each is about 1.5 seconds, some 2.5%. The per-source work dominates: two jobs per due source per tenant, so the queue drains only while `2·N·S·t + 0.003·N ≤ 60`. At one due source per tenant and a one-second upstream that is `N ≤ ~30` tenants; at the client's own ten-second HTTP timeout (`internal/sources/service/factory.go:75`, `Timeout: 10 * time.Second`) it is `N ≤ 3`. At five hundred tenants, one due source each and a one-second upstream, one tick asks for a thousand worker-seconds against sixty — the queue does not drain, and the backlog grows by roughly 940 worker-seconds every tick.\n\n**Why it matters.** Past that ceiling `source.reconcile` falls behind its own schedule and keeps falling further behind, which is the failure `52456fb` was filed about — relocated from a sixty-second timeout to a two-worker queue rather than removed. Reconcile is what discovers that a source is due, so a deployment above the ceiling silently reconciles its sources late, and lateness grows with tenant count. Nothing in the system reports this: there is no metric comparing a periodic's drain to its schedule, which is the same gap ADR 0024 Amendment 3 records for its own reopen triggers.\n\nThe number is also not tunable. `FromPlatformConfig` (`internal/platform/jobs/client.go:88-101`) reads `QueueIngest`, `QueueDefault` — which it spreads across `enrich`, `notify` and `lifecycle` — and `QueueDelivery`, and nothing else; `reconcile` has no configuration knob at all, so an operator who hits this cannot widen it without a rebuild. And the two is a binding SPEC row: `docs/design/SPEC.md:2959-2960` declares `reconcile | 2` for both `source.reconcile` and `silences.sync`, so raising it is a specification change rather than a constant edit. That combination — a capacity limit an operator cannot reach and a number the SPEC pins — is why this was left alone during `52456fb` and written into the doc comment instead.\n\n**Where.** `internal/platform/jobs/client.go:36` (`QueueReconcile: 2`), `:88-101` (`FromPlatformConfig`, which exposes no knob for this queue); `docs/design/SPEC.md:2959-2960` (§G.3's binding table rows for `source.reconcile` and `silences.sync`); `internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:211` (the sixty-second execution timeout); `internal/sources/service/factory.go:75` (the ten-second outbound HTTP timeout that sets the pessimistic case); `internal/sources/service/reconcile.go:663-673` (`FanOut`'s doc comment, where the arithmetic currently lives), `:687` (`FanOut` itself); `internal/app/workers.go` (`reconcileFanOutInterval`, the thirty-second tick). Commit `3124539` and ticket `52456fb` are the conversion that removed the accidental throttle.\n\n**Scope.** medium — the code change is one constant, but it is a production capacity decision that edits a binding SPEC row, and doing it properly means adding the configuration knob that queue has never had.\n\n**Done when.** A decision is recorded about which of three this is: the ceiling is raised, in which case `client.go:36`, SPEC §G.3's two rows and `FanOut`'s doc comment move together and `FromPlatformConfig` grows a `QueueReconcile` knob so an operator can go further without a rebuild; or the ceiling is accepted, in which case the supported tenant count is stated somewhere an operator will find it rather than only in a doc comment on the handler; or the per-source passes are moved off `reconcile` onto a queue whose work is not network-bound, so the tenant walk stops queueing behind third-party latency. Whichever is chosen, `FanOut`'s doc comment stops being the only place the number is written down, and if the ceiling is kept there is something that reports when a deployment has crossed it.","files":null}]}